WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:03.050
- Getting into law school is hard enough.

00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:05.670
But doing well once you get in,

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.930
that is a whole other ball game.

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:10.230
So how did I learn to
do well in law school,

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:13.140
including straight As my first semester?

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:14.533
Wouldn't you like to know.

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:18.223
(upbeat music)

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:22.690
Hey legal eagles, D. James Stone here

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:24.430
teaching you how to think like a lawyer

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:26.540
so you can crush law school.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:28.650
I've been a big firm
lawyer for over 10 years.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:30.520
But before that I went to law school

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:33.000
just like every other attorney.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.020
The thing is, when I got to law school

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:37.360
I completely freaked out.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:40.530
I'd wanted to be a lawyer for
as long as I could remember.

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:42.880
And I knew that, that
meant going to law school.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.960
But nothing could prepare
me for what law school

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:47.850
was actually like.

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:49.230
A lot of kids come into law school

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:51.350
and they're worried because
they don't know anything

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:52.860
about the law.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:54.740
I didn't exactly have that problem

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:57.670
because number one, I
had tons of confidence.

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:00.370
Maybe way too much confidence.

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:04.860
But I'd also had you know,
experience with Mock Trial,

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:08.300
and I'd watched hours and
hours of Law and Order.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:10.306
So I felt like I had, sort of a handle

00:01:10.306 --> 00:01:12.660
on the law itself.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:15.530
But then I got to class
and then I realized

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:18.870
that I knew absolutely
nothing about the law.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:21.940
And not only that but the
quality of the student

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:26.130
in law school is unlike anything
I'd experienced in college.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:28.800
The kids that come from Yale and Harvard

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:31.040
they just have this swagger.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:35.350
And I have never been in
a room with such a high

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:37.580
concentration of just A-type personalities

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:40.820
and people that were just
intellectually stunning.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:44.300
Just so many smart
people all in one place.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:46.450
And then of course, there are the gunners.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:50.610
And a gunner is someone who
always raises their hand,

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:52.750
they're always trying
to answer the questions,

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:54.650
they think they know more than they do

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:58.040
and they think it's their duty to explain

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:00.840
to the professor all of
the things that they know.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.880
They're really obnoxious and
they're really hard to avoid.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:06.770
But the problem is when you're
a first year law student

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:11.500
you can't tell the gunners from
the genuine geniuses either.

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:13.260
They just seem like they
have all the answers.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:16.680
And they seem like they
have the right answers too.

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:19.100
You get to law school
and you're surrounded

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:22.030
by these unbelievably brilliant people.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:23.990
And you're also trying to learn,

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:25.850
essentially a new vocabulary.

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:27.640
You're learning new substantive law

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:29.660
that you've never seen
in your entire life.

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:33.070
And every class basically
has it's own vocabulary,

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:35.110
such that it's like learning
a different language.

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:38.970
But it's like learning
French, German and Spanish

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:43.210
all at the same time in different classes.

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:46.250
So it's just completely overwhelming

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:47.650
when you get to law school.

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:51.000
So my vision for what law
school was going to be like

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.400
did not match up with the reality.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.730
And you know, I tried to do my best.

00:02:55.730 --> 00:02:57.210
I tried to do the readings.

00:02:57.210 --> 00:03:00.790
I tried to engage with the professors.

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:02.740
But there's just so much out there.

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:05.230
And I remember one time in criminal law

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:07.940
where we were discussing
the different forms

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:10.830
of criminal homicide and I thought

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:13.140
I spotted a flaw in the
professor's argument.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:15.780
So I raised my hand and
I explained my argument

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:17.210
and I said you know professor,

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:19.730
isn't that just a semantic difference?

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:21.740
Thinking I was going to win this argument.

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:23.270
And the professor retorted back,

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:26.170
well isn't everything
a semantic difference?

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:28.260
Aren't we always talking
about the differences

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:30.110
between the definitions of words?

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:32.410
And it got a huge laugh
in front of the class

00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:35.100
and I felt humiliated and I didn't realize

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:36.710
I was going to have to be debating with

00:03:36.710 --> 00:03:39.750
essentially a post-modern professor

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:41.440
who was a total relativist.

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:43.100
But, you know, he made a good point.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:46.130
And I'll never forget, you
know, what it felt like

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:49.130
to be skewered at the
end of a logical knife

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:51.410
of this professor.

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:54.620
But that was not a situation
that I was used to being in.

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:57.690
And yet every single day in class

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:01.180
I would find myself on
the horns of a dilemma

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:04.520
or you know, I wouldn't
understand the arguments

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:05.450
that were being made.

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:09.940
Or I felt like I just couldn't
keep up with the readings

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:12.180
and the arguments that
were going on in class.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:15.370
Now, in the past it was
rare that I didn't think

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:18.340
that I was one of the
smartest people in the room.

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:20.920
But in law school I definitely was not

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:23.240
the smartest person in the room.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:25.620
And I probably wasn't
even in the top half.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:28.300
I mean I really felt like I was surrounded

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:30.320
by intellectual geniuses.

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:32.660
And after class it seemed like everyone

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:35.040
was actually studying harder than I was.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:36.670
I don't know how it was possible.

00:04:36.670 --> 00:04:39.370
But they seemed to find more
time in the day to study,

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:42.700
to read their cases, to read supplements.

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:45.170
Everyone was not only really smart

00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:47.440
but no one was slacking off and everyone

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:49.820
was incredibly hard worker.

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:52.960
So I knew that I had to
figure something out.

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:54.571
Because I couldn't just skate by

00:04:54.571 --> 00:04:58.230
with the habits that I had in undergrad

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:01.720
and procrastinating like I used to.

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:04.380
I knew that I was gonna have to have

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:06.570
some different tactic in law school.

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:09.110
Because otherwise I was
just gonna get left behind

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:10.273
by my classmates.

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:14.040
And I knew that I just
couldn't rely on innate talent,

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:17.380
because everyone seemed
to be incredibly talented.

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:20.620
But worse than that everyone
seemed to be working

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:22.480
at least as hard if not harder.

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:25.010
So I knew that I couldn't
rely on innate talent,

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:29.010
and I also couldn't
outwork my cohort either.

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:32.990
I realized I had to come up with a system.

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:35.160
I had to try something different.

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:39.510
So, in addition to studying
the law for my classes,

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:42.320
I also decided to study law school itself.

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:46.293
I figured there must
be a way to go about it

00:05:46.293 --> 00:05:49.300
better and smarter.

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:51.830
So I read everything I possibly could

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:54.860
about the tactics of law school itself.

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:57.220
And I got tips from upperclassman.

00:05:57.220 --> 00:06:00.470
I talked to them about what
worked in their first year

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:01.450
and what didn't work.

00:06:01.450 --> 00:06:03.180
I talk to practicing attorneys.

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:04.780
I really tried to reach out

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:07.080
to get as much on the ground
experience as I could.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:11.480
Because I figured that
experience is the best teacher.

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:13.560
And you know, you only get one shot

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:15.453
at your first year in law school.

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:18.380
So I really had to make the most of it.

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:20.520
And it really seemed like after

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:22.820
you go through your first year,

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:25.200
all of the upperclassman
were completely changed.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:28.340
And so I had to figure out how they made

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:31.300
that transition and how they learned

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:35.610
to do what worked and
discarded what didn't work.

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:39.180
So in addition to reading
about law school itself

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:42.720
I started reading about practice exams.

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:45.460
And not only that but
I tried to do as many

00:06:45.460 --> 00:06:47.730
practice exams as possible.

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:50.740
In those days there used to
be more public data bases

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:54.000
where you could go to
different law school's websites

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:57.400
and you could download more past exams.

00:06:57.400 --> 00:06:59.820
And I figured that there
might be some commonalities

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:01.480
between different exams.

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:04.250
And low and behold I
found that almost everyone

00:07:04.250 --> 00:07:07.610
used this weird, sort of
law school exam called

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:11.190
an Issue Spotting Exam where
you're given a fact pattern.

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:14.800
And what I found is that
Issue Spotting Exams

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:17.450
were really unlike any of the stuff

00:07:17.450 --> 00:07:19.750
that you do in class in law school.

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:23.390
When you're in class
you're basically doing

00:07:23.390 --> 00:07:25.410
appellate opinion autopsies.

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:29.670
So you get an opinion
from an Appellate Court

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:32.420
that lays out the facts
and the legal reasoning

00:07:32.420 --> 00:07:36.030
and comes out with a particular holding.

00:07:36.030 --> 00:07:39.750
And you're basically doing
a dissection or an autopsy

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:41.940
of that opinion.

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:45.290
But the thing is you're
not tested on almost

00:07:45.290 --> 00:07:46.430
any of that information.

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:48.300
The amount of information that you glean

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:51.800
from a particular case is so small

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:56.030
compared to what you
actually do on a final exam.

00:07:56.030 --> 00:07:58.250
And because there are
so many commonalities

00:07:58.250 --> 00:08:03.080
between the final exams,
I figured out what works

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:04.670
for those finals.

00:08:04.670 --> 00:08:09.130
And I learned that the
kind of in class dissection

00:08:09.130 --> 00:08:11.070
that you do on Appellate Opinions,

00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:15.000
well that's important and
you use that in practice.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:18.060
You don't really use it on the final exam.

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:21.100
So I really emphasized my practice exams.

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:24.550
I really emphasized
learning the strategies

00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:29.550
of how to write a really good
Issue Spotting Exam answer.

00:08:30.170 --> 00:08:34.140
Because all the finals are
basically the same format.

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:36.470
And at the end of the
day all the strategies

00:08:36.470 --> 00:08:38.970
that I come up with, all
the experience that I got

00:08:41.537 --> 00:08:43.210
from the upperclassman
and all the experience

00:08:44.233 --> 00:08:45.860
that I had doing my practice exams,

00:08:45.860 --> 00:08:47.180
ended up having a good result.

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:49.510
I remember my first semester

00:08:49.510 --> 00:08:51.650
when I got the report card.

00:08:51.650 --> 00:08:52.970
And it's given to you in an envelope

00:08:52.970 --> 00:08:56.640
and so much relies on those grades.

00:08:56.640 --> 00:09:00.000
I mean you use your first
semester grades to get your,

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:03.170
your 1L summer job when you come back

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:06.620
for your second year to do
interviews with law firms.

00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:10.500
Like, all they look at basically
are your first year grades.

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:13.629
And when I got that first report card,

00:09:13.629 --> 00:09:16.500
I had it in my hand and
my hands were shaking.

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:20.410
And I just couldn't open
it up in the classroom

00:09:20.410 --> 00:09:21.290
that we were given it to.

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:23.360
So I walked outside.

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:25.520
UCLA Law has this really
beautiful courtyard

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:27.880
that's surrounded on
three sides by buildings.

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:29.250
It's got huge trees.

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:32.100
And I sat down on a bench, I opened it up,

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:36.960
and I saw the picket fence, A, A, A.

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:39.460
We only had three classes that semester

00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:41.200
and I got straight As.

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:45.838
And to this day I don't
think I have ever had

00:09:45.838 --> 00:09:49.060
a single better feeling moment

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:53.440
than when I picked up the
phone and I called my parents

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:55.470
and I said, hey mom and dad

00:09:55.470 --> 00:09:57.820
I just got my first semester grades,

00:09:57.820 --> 00:10:00.730
I want you to know I got straight A's.

00:10:00.730 --> 00:10:02.123
Everything's gonna be okay.

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:06.930
And my parents were yelling and crying.

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:10.390
And it was just one of the best moments

00:10:10.390 --> 00:10:11.910
I've ever had in my entire life.

00:10:11.910 --> 00:10:15.720
And as I predicted it would it set me up

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:19.170
for a really good first year job.

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:23.720
It set me up for getting the
big firm job that I wanted

00:10:23.720 --> 00:10:28.720
in Los Angeles and nothing has
really ever compared to that.

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:33.120
And frankly, you know, your
first semester is so important.

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:36.830
It's by far the most
important part of law school.

00:10:36.830 --> 00:10:38.760
And I was able to keep up the momentum.

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:42.853
I graduated with a whole lot more A's.

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:46.800
And the next two and a
half years of law school

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:49.530
were intense but they were nothing like

00:10:49.530 --> 00:10:50.850
that first semester.

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:55.350
I used a lot of those skills
in Mock Trial and Moot Court.

00:10:55.350 --> 00:10:58.740
Where it went on to win a
couple of national tournaments

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:01.905
and between my grades
and my extracurriculars,

00:11:01.905 --> 00:11:04.670
I was able to get set up

00:11:06.700 --> 00:11:08.030
to work for a big firm.

00:11:08.030 --> 00:11:11.930
An Am Law 100 Firm in Los
Angles and I was able to get

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:14.640
my dream job as a Civil Litigator.

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:17.690
And I've been a Litigator ever sense.

00:11:17.690 --> 00:11:22.450
Now, the story of how I
became a big firm lawyer

00:11:22.450 --> 00:11:25.560
is one that we'll have
to save for another day.

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:27.240
But if you wanna hear more stories

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:29.150
about my law school experience,

00:11:29.150 --> 00:11:31.320
or my experience as an attorney,

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:33.630
check out this short
playlist I put together.

00:11:33.630 --> 00:11:34.463
It has a bunch of legal war stories

00:11:34.463 --> 00:11:38.780
and I think you'll
really enjoy the videos.

00:11:38.780 --> 00:11:41.780
So click on this playlist and
I'll see ya in the next video.

